23
00:01:46,538 --> 00:01:48,243
So why agents break monitoring?

24
00:01:49,728 --> 00:01:52,736
Agents don't just run, they act.

25
00:01:53,797 --> 00:01:58,524
For twenty years, our first monitoring
question has been, "Is it running?"

26
00:01:59,371 --> 00:02:04,210
And for a normal service, that
question gets you most of the way.

27
00:02:05,794 --> 00:02:07,024
But agents are different.

28
00:02:07,838 --> 00:02:10,215
An agent doesn't just respond.

29
00:02:10,805 --> 00:02:16,086
It reasons over data, decides
which tool to call, read the

30
00:02:16,086 --> 00:02:18,490
result, and act on the world.

31
00:02:20,339 --> 00:02:23,067
Look at the loop on the right.

32
00:02:24,072 --> 00:02:29,990
Reason, call to tool, observe, act,
and then maybe go around again.

33
00:02:30,870 --> 00:02:33,747
The keyword is non-deterministic.

34
00:02:34,464 --> 00:02:40,637
The same question can take two steps today
and maybe seven stop-- steps tomorrow.

35
00:02:41,598 --> 00:02:45,413
So is it running is no longer
the interesting question.

36
00:02:47,146 --> 00:02:52,212
The question that matter now
are on the left or the right?

37
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:53,891
What it is doing?

38
00:02:54,605 --> 00:02:55,993
Which tool is it invoking?

39
00:02:56,664 --> 00:02:58,903
And are those the right ones?

40
00:02:59,572 --> 00:03:01,030
How are the models behaving?

41
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:07,621
Latency, errors, throttling, stop
reasons, and what does each interaction

42
00:03:07,791 --> 00:03:13,487
cost Traditional monitoring answers
the first question well, but it

43
00:03:13,510 --> 00:03:15,492
barely touches the other four

44
00:03:16,492 --> 00:03:22,850
Here's what that looks like in real life
So you have the dashboard at 3:00 a.m.

45
00:03:23,562 --> 00:03:28,992
P95 latency is fine, error rate is 0.2%,

46
00:03:29,385 --> 00:03:32,783
Lambda is healthy, CPU is at 34%.

47
00:03:33,365 --> 00:03:35,468
Everything looks solid green.

48
00:03:37,288 --> 00:03:40,365
Now look at what's actually
happening underneath.

49
00:03:41,014 --> 00:03:44,969
The agent is looping, calling
the same tool again and again.

50
00:03:45,709 --> 00:03:50,005
It's passing hallucinated
parameters, a perfect valid

51
00:03:50,012 --> 00:03:52,234
call with the wrong arguments.

52
00:03:53,124 --> 00:03:57,250
The conversation is drifting slowly,
moving away from what the user asked.

53
00:03:58,274 --> 00:04:04,773
Tokens are burning, and you will
find out a $2,000 bill, not an alert.

54
00:04:05,905 --> 00:04:10,520
And worst of all, the agent
takes misaligned action.

55
00:04:11,438 --> 00:04:14,033
It succeed, but it does the wrong thing.

56
00:04:15,033 --> 00:04:19,629
None of these five raise
traditional alert, not a single one

57
00:04:20,629 --> 00:04:25,136
Health infrastructure is not the
same as a healthy agent behavior

58
00:04:26,136 --> 00:04:27,836
So what do we capture instead?

59
00:04:28,661 --> 00:04:33,748
Metrics, logs, and traces
still matter, but agents need

60
00:04:34,152 --> 00:04:36,031
six additional signals now.

61
00:04:36,896 --> 00:04:39,407
One, the agent decision trace.

62
00:04:40,544 --> 00:04:43,264
Why did the agent choose this next step?

63
00:04:44,316 --> 00:04:46,727
Two, tooling location spans.

64
00:04:47,452 --> 00:04:52,275
Every external call with its
latency, inputs, and outputs.

65
00:04:53,293 --> 00:04:57,268
Three, token cost telemetry.

66
00:04:57,886 --> 00:05:03,272
Prompt and completion tokens, and
the cost of every step And then

67
00:05:03,673 --> 00:05:09,576
model behavior signals, stop reasons,
errors, throttles, and safety events.

68
00:05:10,675 --> 00:05:14,062
The fifth one is model behavior signals

69
00:05:15,062 --> 00:05:16,720
The session and memory state.

70
00:05:17,491 --> 00:05:19,527
What does the agent remember right now?

71
00:05:19,778 --> 00:05:22,603
Which is a very important
questions to answer.

72
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:26,527
And finally, the guardrail evaluations.

73
00:05:27,351 --> 00:05:30,102
Did the safety check fire, and why?

74
00:05:31,096 --> 00:05:36,333
The good news is Datadog LLM
observability models most of these

75
00:05:36,374 --> 00:05:41,830
as spans, the same mental model you
already use for distributed tracing.

