373
00:28:15,916 --> 00:28:18,216
Agents run on production traffic.

374
00:28:18,734 --> 00:28:21,969
Telemetry flows out as
spans, metrics and logs.

375
00:28:22,838 --> 00:28:25,737
Monitors apply thresholds
and anomaly detection.

376
00:28:26,319 --> 00:28:32,262
If something's wrong, they page on
call and trip the circuit breaker.

377
00:28:33,952 --> 00:28:35,725
What should be the monitors watch?

378
00:28:36,681 --> 00:28:44,005
Cost per session, tool call rates, burst
or repeated of the same tool, error

379
00:28:44,096 --> 00:28:46,343
and throttle rate across the model.

380
00:28:46,810 --> 00:28:51,797
The tools downstream services and
guardrails hits a sudden spike

381
00:28:51,861 --> 00:28:55,926
or silent drops, which can mean
a guardrail has stopped working

382
00:28:56,926 --> 00:28:58,420
In the demo, this was real.

383
00:28:58,711 --> 00:29:03,910
An alarm on tool call rate more than four
calls a minute invokes a Lambda that flip

384
00:29:03,954 --> 00:29:06,912
SSM parameter from the architecture slide.

385
00:29:07,497 --> 00:29:12,480
Every tool checks it, and for 90
seconds, every tool return suspense

386
00:29:13,480 --> 00:29:17,097
To owner's limits, it's
global, so it pause every

387
00:29:17,097 --> 00:29:19,256
user, not just one who trigger.

388
00:29:19,964 --> 00:29:23,246
And because the alarm evaluate
once a minute, it stop the next

389
00:29:23,247 --> 00:29:25,570
turn, not the one that tripped it.

390
00:29:26,494 --> 00:29:31,209
The golden rule, build the kill switch
before the agent goes into production

391
00:29:32,209 --> 00:29:37,762
Finally, treat the agent like any
other service with SLIs and SLOs

392
00:29:39,152 --> 00:29:40,581
and five reliability questions.

393
00:29:41,296 --> 00:29:42,737
Does the agent finish the job?

394
00:29:43,422 --> 00:29:47,530
Me-measure task success, no
error, guardrails passed.

395
00:29:48,352 --> 00:29:50,443
For example, ninety-nine
percent of sessions.

396
00:29:51,417 --> 00:29:52,332
Is it fast enough?

397
00:29:52,874 --> 00:29:55,414
P95 end-to-end latency per session.

398
00:29:56,023 --> 00:29:58,097
For example, under five seconds.

399
00:29:58,875 --> 00:30:04,591
And remember the trace from our previous
slide that time is mostly the model.

400
00:30:06,116 --> 00:30:07,546
Do its tools behave?

401
00:30:08,284 --> 00:30:10,869
Tool call error rate
and calls per session.

402
00:30:11,932 --> 00:30:13,189
Errors under one percent.

403
00:30:14,276 --> 00:30:15,965
Is, is spend under control?

404
00:30:16,394 --> 00:30:20,345
Tokens, cost per session
within twice the baseline.

405
00:30:21,178 --> 00:30:22,399
Are guardrails working?

406
00:30:23,036 --> 00:30:25,188
Guardrail trigger and block rate.

407
00:30:25,608 --> 00:30:28,189
Alert on deviation in either direction.

408
00:30:29,139 --> 00:30:34,140
These targets are example, turn them
to your workload and alert on error

409
00:30:34,155 --> 00:30:36,971
budget burn rate, not singly spikes

410
00:30:37,971 --> 00:30:42,586
Four lessons that keep repeating across
enterprise deployments, which I have seen.

411
00:30:43,590 --> 00:30:45,097
Instrument before you deploy.

412
00:30:46,097 --> 00:30:49,726
Retrofitting telemetry after
an incident means flying blind

413
00:30:50,055 --> 00:30:52,175
exactly when you need data most.

414
00:30:53,119 --> 00:30:55,114
Propagate trace context early.

415
00:30:55,892 --> 00:31:01,363
Multi-agent systems and tool hops
only stitch into one trace if the

416
00:31:01,366 --> 00:31:03,380
contract travels with every call.

417
00:31:05,224 --> 00:31:06,790
Observe the guardrails too.

418
00:31:07,487 --> 00:31:12,113
A misconfigured guardrail is
invisible unless its decisions

419
00:31:12,115 --> 00:31:13,552
are recorded as telemetry.

420
00:31:14,260 --> 00:31:19,269
That's why the escalation in the demo is
a span attribute, not just a log line.

421
00:31:20,220 --> 00:31:23,273
And treat tokens as a production metric.

422
00:31:23,834 --> 00:31:28,137
Unmonitored token burn can
break the business case long

423
00:31:28,140 --> 00:31:29,544
before it break the service

424
00:31:30,544 --> 00:31:36,241
So moving to key takeaways, let
me leave you with four takeaways.

425
00:31:37,187 --> 00:31:42,311
